## Why This Is Asked

This tests CGOA cross-language callback wiring, memory ownership, and concurrency under real streaming workloads.

## Key Concepts

- CGO callback bridging and //export
- Per-chunk memory ownership and copies into Go strings
- Safe concurrency with worker pools and callback lifetimes
- Error propagation from Go to C and back

## Code Example

```c
// header
void stream_process(const uint8_t* data, size_t len, void (*cb)(const char* chunk, size_t clen, void* user), void* user);
```

```c
// minimal impl
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>

void stream_process(const uint8_t* data, size_t len, void (*cb)(const char*, size_t, void*), void* user) {
  size_t i, n = (len + 63) / 64;
  for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    size_t offset = i * 64;
    size_t chunk_len = (offset + 64 <= len) ? 64 : (len - offset);
    cb((const char*)(data + offset), chunk_len, user);
  }
}
```

```go
// binding sketch
//export goChunk
func goChunk(cChunk *C.char, clen C.size_t, user unsafe.Pointer) {
  b := C.GoBytes(unsafe.Pointer(cChunk), C.int(clen))
